#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709386 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(1 - y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- x + 2 y + 2\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. The diagram shows three piecewise-smooth directed paths: $\\gamma$ goes from $B$ to $A$, $\\beta$ goes from $C$ to $B$, $\\alpha$ goes from $C$ to $A$.\n\n![gamma from B to A; beta from C to B; alpha from C to A](images/2f/2fb36289f8cdfbc6f8d09ffe604eab58156c31434cbf8d8fa738b1aecb142134.png)\n\nFor a directed path $\\eta$, write $W_\\eta=\\int_\\eta\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. You are given $W_{\\gamma}=-1$ and $W_{\\alpha}=-3$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=1 - y$ and $Q=- x + 2 y + 2$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=-1\\\\Q_x&=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey agree everywhere. The components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. Follow the route $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$. Relative to the displayed arrows, its edge signs give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-W_{\\gamma}-W_{\\beta}+W_{\\alpha}&=0\\\\-W_{\\beta}&=2\\\\W_{\\beta}&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReversing an edge changes its work sign. The proposed cycle sum evaluates to $0$. Compare the proposed missing work with $-2$.\n\n- $W_{\\beta}=-4$. This is **false**.\n- $-W_{\\gamma}-W_{\\beta}+W_{\\alpha}=0$. This is **true**.\n- $\\oint_L\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some closed path $L$ in $\\mathbb R^2$. This is **false**.\n- $\\mathbf F$ is **not** conservative. This is **false**.\n- None of the above This is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709558 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the straight segment from $A=(0,0)$ to $B=(1,-1)$. Let $f(x,y)=e^{x+y}$. Which definite integral is equivalent to $\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The endpoint position vectors give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(t)&=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)\\\\\n&=\\langle t,- t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ and $t=1$ this gives $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(1,-1)$, respectively.\n\nDifferentiate, compute the speed, and substitute both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 1,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(1)^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{2},\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=e^{(t)+(- t)}\\\\\n&=e^{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the scalar arc-length formula,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(e^{0}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709566 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the straight segment from $A=(-2,2)$ to $B=(-4,2)$. Let $f(x,y)=x+\\sin y$. Which definite integral is equivalent to $\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The endpoint position vectors give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(t)&=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 t - 2,2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ and $t=1$ this gives $A=(-2,2)$ and $B=(-4,2)$, respectively.\n\nDifferentiate, compute the speed, and substitute both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle -2,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+(0)^2}=2,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(- 2 t - 2)+\\sin\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 t - 2+\\sin\\left(2\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the scalar arc-length formula,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\\\\\n&=2\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 2 t - 2+\\sin\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709572 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the straight segment from $A=(0,0)$ to $B=(2,2)$. Let $f(x,y)=x^2-y$. Which definite integral is equivalent to $\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The endpoint position vectors give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(t)&=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 t,2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ and $t=1$ this gives $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(2,2)$, respectively.\n\nDifferentiate, compute the speed, and substitute both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2)^2+(2)^2}=2 \\sqrt{2},\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(2 t)^2-(2 t)\\\\\n&=4 t^{2} - 2 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the scalar arc-length formula,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\\\\\n&=2 \\sqrt{2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(4 t^{2} - 2 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709573 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the straight segment from $A=(0,0)$ to $B=(2,-2)$. Let $f(x,y)=2x+y$. Which definite integral is equivalent to $\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The endpoint position vectors give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(t)&=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 t,- 2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ and $t=1$ this gives $A=(0,0)$ and $B=(2,-2)$, respectively.\n\nDifferentiate, compute the speed, and substitute both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2,-2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2)^2+(-2)^2}=2 \\sqrt{2},\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=2(2 t)+(- 2 t)\\\\\n&=2 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the scalar arc-length formula,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\\\\\n&=2 \\sqrt{2}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709588 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the straight segment from $A=(-1,-1)$ to $B=(-1,-2)$. Let $f(x,y)=1+x-\\cos y$. Which definite integral is equivalent to $\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The endpoint position vectors give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(t)&=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)\\\\\n&=\\langle -1,- t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ and $t=1$ this gives $A=(-1,-1)$ and $B=(-1,-2)$, respectively.\n\nDifferentiate, compute the speed, and substitute both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 0,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-1)^2}=1,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=1+(-1)-\\cos\\left(- t - 1\\right)\\\\\n&=0-\\cos\\left(- t - 1\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the scalar arc-length formula,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(0-\\cos\\left(- t - 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709829 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ traversed along the line segment from $(-6,2,6)$ to $(-5,4,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(t - 6,2 t + 2,6 - 2 t\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(-6,2,6\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(-5,4,4\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(1,2,-2\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(1)^2+(2)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{(2 t + 2)+(6 - 2 t)}{t - 6} \\\\  &=\\frac{8}{t - 6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{8}{t - 6}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709838 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ traversed along the line segment from $(4,-2,4)$ to $(3,0,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(4 - t,2 t - 2,4 - 2 t\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(4,-2,4\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(3,0,2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(-1,2,-2\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(2)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{(2 t - 2)+(4 - 2 t)}{4 - t} \\\\  &=- \\frac{2}{t - 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(- \\frac{2}{t - 4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709847 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y, z) = x + y + z$, traversed along the line segment from $(-4, 2, 0)$ to $(-2, 3, 3)$?" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(2 t - 4,t + 2,3 t\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(-4,2,0\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(-2,3,3\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(2,1,3\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2)^2+(1)^2+(3)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(2 t - 4)+(t + 2)+(3 t) \\\\  &=6 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{14}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6 t - 2)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709850 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ traversed along the line segment from $(-5,2,6)$ to $(-3,0,8)?$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(2 t - 5,2 - 2 t,2 t + 6\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(-5,2,6\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(-3,0,8\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(2,-2,2\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2)^2+(-2)^2+(2)^2} \\\\  &=2 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{(2 - 2 t)+(2 t + 6)}{2 t - 5} \\\\  &=\\frac{8}{2 t - 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=2 \\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{8}{2 t - 5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709852 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x},$ traversed along the line segment from $(2,-3,5)$ to $(1,-1,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(2 - t,2 t - 3,5 - 2 t\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(2,-3,5\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(1,-1,3\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(-1,2,-2\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(2)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{(2 t - 3)+(5 - 2 t)}{2 - t} \\\\  &=- \\frac{2}{t - 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(- \\frac{2}{t - 2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709876 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(4,4,3)$ to $(2,3,6).$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(4 - 2 t,4 - t,3 t + 3\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(4,4,3\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(2,3,6\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(-2,-1,3\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+(-1)^2+(3)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 - 2 t)(4 - t)^2 \\\\  &=- 2 t^{3} + 20 t^{2} - 64 t + 64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the expanded polynomial and subtract the endpoint values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{14}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(- 2 t^{3} + 20 t^{2} - 64 t + 64)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\left[- \\frac{t^{4}}{2} + \\frac{20 t^{3}}{3} - 32 t^{2} + 64 t\\right]_0^1 \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\left(\\left(\\frac{229}{6}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &=\\frac{229 \\sqrt{14}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709878 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(2,3,-1)$ to $(3,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(t + 2,3 - 2 t,t - 1\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(2,3,-1\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(3,1,0\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(1,-2,1\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(1)^2+(-2)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(t + 2)(3 - 2 t)^2 \\\\  &=4 t^{3} - 4 t^{2} - 15 t + 18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the expanded polynomial and subtract the endpoint values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4 t^{3} - 4 t^{2} - 15 t + 18)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &=\\sqrt{6}\\left[t^{4} - \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - \\frac{15 t^{2}}{2} + 18 t\\right]_0^1 \\\\  &=\\sqrt{6}\\left(\\left(\\frac{61}{6}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &=\\frac{61 \\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709879 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(3,3,2)$ to $(2,2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(3 - t,3 - t,2 t + 2\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(3,3,2\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(2,2,4\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(-1,-1,2\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-1)^2+(2)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 - t)(3 - t)^2 \\\\  &=- t^{3} + 9 t^{2} - 27 t + 27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the expanded polynomial and subtract the endpoint values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(- t^{3} + 9 t^{2} - 27 t + 27)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &=\\sqrt{6}\\left[- \\frac{t^{4}}{4} + 3 t^{3} - \\frac{27 t^{2}}{2} + 27 t\\right]_0^1 \\\\  &=\\sqrt{6}\\left(\\left(\\frac{65}{4}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &=\\frac{65 \\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709891 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(4,2,2)$ to $(1,3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(4 - 3 t,t + 2,2 - 2 t\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(4,2,2\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(1,3,0\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(-3,1,-2\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(1)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 - 3 t)(t + 2)^2 \\\\  &=- 3 t^{3} - 8 t^{2} + 4 t + 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the expanded polynomial and subtract the endpoint values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{14}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(- 3 t^{3} - 8 t^{2} + 4 t + 16)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\left[- \\frac{3 t^{4}}{4} - \\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} + 2 t^{2} + 16 t\\right]_0^1 \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\left(\\left(\\frac{175}{12}\\right)-(0)\\right) \\\\  &=\\frac{175 \\sqrt{14}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709902 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(-2,4,-4)$ to $(-3,6,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(- t - 2,2 t + 4,2 t - 4\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(-2,4,-4\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(-3,6,-2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(-1,2,2\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(2)^2+(2)^2} \\\\  &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}((- t - 2)+(2 t - 4))\\right) \\\\  &=\\cos{\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2} \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2} \\right)})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $u=\\frac{\\pi t}{4} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and $\\mathrm{d}u=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The new bounds are $u(0)=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and $u(1)=- \\frac{5 \\pi}{4}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I&=\\frac{12}{\\pi}\\int_{- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{5 \\pi}{4}}\\cos u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &=\\frac{12}{\\pi}\\left[\\sin u\\right]_{- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{5 \\pi}{4}} \\\\  &=\\frac{12}{\\pi}\\left(\\sin\\left(- \\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\right)-\\sin\\left(- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  &=\\frac{12}{\\pi}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-(1)\\right) \\\\  &=\\frac{6 \\left(-2 + \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{\\pi}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709913 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(3,1,1)$ to $(7,3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(4 t + 3,2 t + 1,t + 1\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(3,1,1\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(7,3,2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(4,2,1\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(4)^2+(2)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 t + 3)e^{(2 t + 1)(t + 1)} \\\\  &=\\left(4 t + 3\\right) e^{2 t^{2} + 3 t + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\left(4 t + 3\\right) e^{2 t^{2} + 3 t + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $u=2 t^{2} + 3 t + 1$ and $\\mathrm{d}u=(4 t + 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The new bounds are $u(0)=1$ and $u(1)=6$. Since $x(t)=1\\,u^{\\prime}(t)$, the differential contributes the factor $1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I&=\\sqrt{21}\\int_{1}^{6}e^u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &=\\sqrt{21}\\left[e^u\\right]_{1}^{6} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{21}\\left(e^{6}-e^{1}\\right) \\\\  &=\\sqrt{21}\\left(e^{6}-(e)\\right) \\\\  &=\\sqrt{21} \\left(- e + e^{6}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709914 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(0,0,0)$ to $(-8,-2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(- 8 t,- 2 t,- t\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(0,0,0\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(-8,-2,-1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(-8,-2,-1\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-8)^2+(-2)^2+(-1)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{69}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(- 8 t)e^{(- 2 t)(- t)} \\\\  &=- 8 t e^{2 t^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{69}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(- 8 t e^{2 t^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $u=2 t^{2}$ and $\\mathrm{d}u=(4 t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The new bounds are $u(0)=0$ and $u(1)=2$. Since $x(t)=-2\\,u^{\\prime}(t)$, the differential contributes the factor $-2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I&=- 2 \\sqrt{69}\\int_{0}^{2}e^u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &=- 2 \\sqrt{69}\\left[e^u\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\  &=- 2 \\sqrt{69}\\left(e^{2}-e^{0}\\right) \\\\  &=- 2 \\sqrt{69}\\left(e^{2}-(1)\\right) \\\\  &=- 2 \\sqrt{69} \\left(-1 + e^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710815 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $xy+y^2=2x^2+1.$" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$. The product rule gives $(xy)'=y+xy'$, and the chain rule gives $(y^2)'=2yy'$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+xy'+2yy'&=4x\\\\\nxy'+2yy'&=4x-y\\\\\n(x+2y)y'&=4x-y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by the coefficient of $y'$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{4x-y}{x+2y},\\qquad x+2y\\ne0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710816 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $x^2\\sin y+y^2=xe^y+1.$" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Both sides contain a product. Differentiate each product, retaining the chain-rule factor $y'$ when differentiating $\\sin y$ or $e^y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(x^2\\sin y)&=2x\\sin y+x^2\\cos y\\,y',\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(xe^y)&=e^y+xe^y y'.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, then move every term containing $y'$ to the left and the other terms to the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x\\sin y+x^2\\cos y\\,y'+2yy'&=e^y+xe^y y'\\\\\nx^2\\cos y\\,y'+2yy'-xe^y y'&=e^y-2x\\sin y\\\\\n(x^2\\cos y+2y-xe^y)y'&=e^y-2x\\sin y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{e^y-2x\\sin y}{x^2\\cos y+2y-xe^y}.\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $x^2\\cos y+2y-xe^y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710822 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $x\\sin y+y^2=x^3+1.$" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Apply the product rule to $x\\sin y$ and the chain rule to each function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(x\\sin y)&=\\sin y+x\\cos y\\,y',\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(y^2)&=2yy'.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the equation and collecting the terms containing $y'$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin y+x\\cos y\\,y'+2yy'&=3x^2\\\\\nx\\cos y\\,y'+2yy'&=3x^2-\\sin y\\\\\n(x\\cos y+2y)y'&=3x^2-\\sin y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{3x^2-\\sin y}{x\\cos y+2y},\\qquad x\\cos y+2y\\ne0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710910 :question/problem "Given that $y^2-3x+y=1$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. The chain rule gives $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(y^2)=2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(y^2-3x+y)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(1)\\\\\n2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-3+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=3\\\\\n(2y+1)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=3\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{3}{2y+1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $2y+1\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710915 :question/problem "Given that $2^y-y=x^2+1$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. For the exponential term, use the general-base rule together with the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(2^y)=2^y\\ln(2)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(2^y-y)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(x^2+1)\\\\\n2^y\\ln(2)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactor out $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2^y\\ln(2)-1)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2x\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{2x}{2^y\\ln(2)-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $2^y\\ln(2)-1\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710918 :question/problem "Given that $y^3+\\cos(y)-x^2=2$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. Both $y^3$ and $\\cos(y)$ require a factor of $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ from the chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(y^3+\\cos(y)-x^2)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(2)\\\\\n3y^2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-\\sin(y)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-2x&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect and factor the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3y^2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-\\sin(y)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2x\\\\\n(3y^2-\\sin(y))\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2x\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{2x}{3y^2-\\sin(y)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $3y^2-\\sin(y)\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711006 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ for the curve $xe^{xy}+y^2=e+1.$ Give an exact answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The term $xe^{xy}$ needs the product rule. Inside the exponential, $xy$ also needs the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy)=y+x\\frac{dy}{dx},\\qquad\n\\frac{d}{dx}(e^{xy})=e^{xy}\\left(y+x\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the curve and collect all terms containing $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xe^{xy}+y^2)&=\\frac{d}{dx}(e+1)\\\\\ne^{xy}+xe^{xy}\\left(y+x\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0\\\\\ne^{xy}+xye^{xy}+x^2e^{xy}\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0\\\\\n(x^2e^{xy}+2y)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-e^{xy}(1+xy)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{e^{xy}(1+xy)}{x^2e^{xy}+2y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(1,1)$, $e^{xy}=e$ and the denominator $e+2$ is positive. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(1,1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{e(1+1)}{e+2}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2e}{e+2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711009 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(2,1)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=x^2-1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function of $x$. Use the product rule on $xy$ and the chain rule on $y^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy+y^2)&=\\frac{d}{dx}(x^2-1)\\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}+y+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}&=2x\\\\\n(x+2y)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=2x-y\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=\\frac{2x-y}{x+2y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(2,1)$, the denominator is $2+2(1)=4$, so the slope is finite. Substituting gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(2,1)}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2(2)-1}{2+2(1)}\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711015 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,2)$ for the curve $3x^2+2y^3=19.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, differentiate the curve implicitly with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function of $x$. The chain rule supplies a factor of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ when differentiating $y^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(3x^2+2y^3)&=\\frac{d}{dx}(19)\\\\\n6x+6y^2\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0\\\\\n6y^2\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-6x\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{x}{y^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the derivative at $(1,2)$:\n\n$$\nm=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(1,2)}=-\\frac{1}{2^2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711063 :question/problem "The circle $x^2+y^2=25$ has a tangent at $(3,4)$ that meets the $y$-axis at $P$.\n\n![Circle with the tangent through (3,4) meeting the y-axis at P.](images/3b/3b7add027c857979a54396568b0c0a6928060f97b1af2e2a6b8520bebb025ec0.png)\n\nImplicit differentiation has been started for you:\n\n$$\n2x+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}=0.\n$$\n\nFind the coordinates of $P$. Use the tangent slope in $y-4=m(x-3)$, and remember that $x=0$ on the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Solve the given differentiated equation for the tangent slope:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nAt $(3,4)$, the tangent slope is $m=-\\frac{3}{4}$. The tangent line is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-4&=-\\frac{3}{4}(x-3) \\\\\ny&=-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{25}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=0$ to find its intersection with the $y$-axis. This gives $y=\\frac{25}{4}$, so\n\n$$\nP=\\left(0,\\frac{25}{4}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711064 :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $xy+y^2=6$. The normal line at $(1,2)$ on the curve intersects the $y$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$. Give exact values.\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function of $x$. The product rule gives $\\frac{d}{dx}(xy)=y+x\\frac{dy}{dx}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+x\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n(x+2y)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{y}{x+2y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{2}{1+2(2)}=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe normal slope is its negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{n}}=-\\frac{1}{m_{\\mathrm{t}}}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe normal through $(1,2)$ has equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-2&=\\frac{5}{2}(x-1) \\\\\ny&=\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=0$ for the $y$-axis intersection. Then $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$, giving\n\n$$\nP=\\left(0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711079 :question/problem "The equation $x^2+2y^2=17$ represents an ellipse. The tangent line at $(3,2)$ on the ellipse intersects the $x$-axis at $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?\n\n![Ellipse with the tangent through (3,2) meeting the x-axis at P.](images/d3/d3a242f9c327e1ff42e8c6bb36b13a02ddf41ef4382851f53ae4805fb8deaa96.png)" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and differentiate implicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x+4y\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{x}{2y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(3,2)$, the tangent slope is $m=-\\frac{3}{4}$. Using this slope and the given point gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-2&=-\\frac{3}{4}(x-3) \\\\\ny&=-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{17}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{17}{4} \\\\\n3x&=17 \\\\\nx&=\\frac{17}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(\\frac{17}{3},0\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711252 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $3 x^{2} e^{y} + y^{2}=x^{3} - 1$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{2} e^{y}\\right)&=3 x^{2} y' e^{y} + 6 x e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 x e^{y} + y' \\left(3 x^{2} e^{y} + 2 y\\right)&=3 x^{2}\\\\\n\\left(3 x^{2} e^{y} + 2 y\\right)y^{\\prime}&=3 x^{2} - 6 x e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{3 x^{2} - 6 x e^{y}}{3 x^{2} e^{y} + 2 y}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $3 x^{2} e^{y} + 2 y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711276 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $3 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y^{3}=2 x^{2} - 1$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=- 3 x^{3} y' \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 9 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + y' \\left(- 3 x^{3} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 9 y^{2}\\right)&=4 x\\\\\n\\left(- 3 x^{3} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 9 y^{2}\\right)y^{\\prime}&=- 9 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 4 x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{- 9 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 4 x}{- 3 x^{3} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 9 y^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $- 3 x^{3} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 9 y^{2}\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711277 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $2 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y=x^{2}$. This relation requires $y>0$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(2 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=\\frac{2 x y'}{y} + 2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny' \\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} - 3\\right) + 2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}&=2 x\\\\\n\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} - 3\\right)y^{\\prime}&=2 x - 2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{2 x - 2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}}{\\frac{2 x}{y} - 3}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $\\frac{2 x}{y} - 3\\ne0$. This relation requires $y>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711280 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $2 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + y^{3}=3 x^{3} + 1$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(2 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=2 x^{2} y' \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 4 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + y' \\left(2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 3 y^{2}\\right)&=9 x^{2}\\\\\n\\left(2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 3 y^{2}\\right)y^{\\prime}&=9 x^{2} - 4 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{9 x^{2} - 4 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}{2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 3 y^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 3 y^{2}\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711282 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - y^{3}=3 x^{3} - 1$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=- 3 x^{2} y' \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 6 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + y' \\left(- 3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y^{2}\\right)&=9 x^{2}\\\\\n\\left(- 3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y^{2}\\right)y^{\\prime}&=9 x^{2} - 6 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{9 x^{2} - 6 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}}{- 3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $- 3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y^{2}\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711289 :question/problem "Given that $y^{2} + y-(- 4 x)=-2$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(y^{2} + y\\right)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- 4 x - 2\\right)\\\\\n2 y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+1\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 y + 1\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-4\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-\\frac{4}{2 y + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies on real branches where $2 y + 1\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711292 :question/problem "Given that $y^{2} + 2 y-(- 4 x)=2$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(y^{2} + 2 y\\right)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(2 - 4 x\\right)\\\\\n2 y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 y + 2\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-4\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-\\frac{4}{2 y + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies on real branches where $2 y + 2\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711296 :question/problem "Given that $y^{2} + 2 y-(4 x)=0$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(y^{2} + 2 y\\right)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(4 x\\right)\\\\\n2 y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 y + 2\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=4\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{4}{2 y + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies on real branches where $2 y + 2\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711298 :question/problem "Given that $y^{2} - 2 y=3 x - 3$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(y^{2} - 2 y\\right)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x - 3\\right)\\\\\n2 y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 y - 2\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=3\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{3}{2 y - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies on real branches where $2 y - 2\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711313 :question/problem "Given that $y^{2} - y-(4 x)=-2$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(y^{2} - y\\right)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(4 x - 2\\right)\\\\\n2 y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-1\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 y - 1\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=4\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{4}{2 y - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies on real branches where $2 y - 1\\ne 0$."}]}
